<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Saturday (1929)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Black Saturday was the shooting of a peaceful Mau procession by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, Western Samoa, on 28 December 1929. About eleven Samoans were killed, among them the Mau leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a>, who urged his followers not to retaliate. The killings strengthened the non-violent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau movement</a> for Samoan self-government.</p></div>
